# Line endings.
#
# core.autocrlf is true on the Windows development box, so everything checks out
# CRLF here. That is fine for the .bat files and harmless for source - and FATAL
# for anything the Linux box executes, because a shebang read as `bash\r` fails
# with the memorable and unhelpful:
#
#     /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# That cost a deploy on 21 Aug. Shell scripts are pinned to LF so they survive
# the trip to the box regardless of anyone's local git config.
*.sh    text eol=lf

# Batch files are only ever run by cmd.exe, which wants CRLF.
*.bat    text eol=crlf

# The web root is rsynced to a Linux box and served as-is. Keeping these LF means
# the bytes on disk here and the bytes Caddy serves are identical, which is what
# lets deploy/verify-live.ps1 compare them by hash.
*.js     text eol=lf
*.css    text eol=lf
*.html   text eol=lf
*.py     text eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf
*.json   text eol=lf
